1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,312
Most FPGA projects describe their hardware twice: once for the chip and once for the tools. bob describes it once, in data, and generates everything else.

2
00:00:11,867 --> 00:00:16,711
Everything about bob's architecture is one Python file, device.py.

3
00:00:17,031 --> 00:00:28,145
It says how big the grid is, which tile types exist and which configuration fields each one has, where the memory and multiplier columns go, and which pad is which switch or LED.

4
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:42,536
From it come the device file every tool reads, the Verilog parameters, the fabric itself, the architecture file for the VPR router, the names of every configuration feature, and through them the Python models and the board checks.

5
00:00:42,856 --> 00:00:46,952
Two copies of a number is a bug waiting to happen; here there is one.

6
00:00:48,533 --> 00:00:53,932
Here is the grid that description produces: 14 columns by 12 rows of tiles.

7
00:00:54,252 --> 00:00:58,348
The outer ring is 44 I/O pads, and the corners are empty.

8
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:08,091
Inside, the columns are logic blocks, except column 3, which holds block RAM, and column 8, which holds DSP slices.

9
00:01:08,411 --> 00:01:15,717
A memory or a multiplier is taller than a logic block, so each one spans 5 rows, and each column holds two.

10
00:01:16,037 --> 00:01:19,088
That leaves exactly ten by ten logic blocks.

11
00:01:19,567 --> 00:01:25,183
The board's switches and buttons sit on fixed pads on the west edge, and the LEDs on the east edge.

12
00:01:25,503 --> 00:01:31,985
Every other pad has no wire to the outside world: it is reached only through boundary scan, over JTAG.

13
00:01:33,567 --> 00:01:39,769
device.py also writes an architecture file for VPR, the academic place-and-route tool.

14
00:01:40,089 --> 00:01:50,506
It describes each tile's pins: a logic block has sixteen inputs from the routing, eight outputs back to it, a carry in and out, and clock enable, reset and clock.

15
00:01:50,967 --> 00:02:03,683
And it describes the wires: channels 36 tracks wide, made of unidirectional wires that each span 4 tiles, joined by Wilton switch boxes, with ten percent of the tracks reachable from each pin.

16
00:02:04,003 --> 00:02:09,573
These numbers follow OpenFPGA's reference architecture, and chapter four draws them.

17
00:02:11,133 --> 00:02:15,011
Here is the central idea, borrowed from OpenFPGA.

18
00:02:15,331 --> 00:02:26,677
Instead of inventing a routing network and hoping the router can use it, bob asks VPR to build its routing resource graph from the architecture file, once, and commits that graph.

19
00:02:27,133 --> 00:02:29,409
Then fabric_gen walks the graph.

20
00:02:29,729 --> 00:02:37,569
Every node that has more than one possible driver becomes a multiplexer in Verilog, and its select bits become configuration bits.

21
00:02:37,889 --> 00:02:40,951
The result is compiled by Vivado into the host.

22
00:02:41,433 --> 00:02:44,928
So the router's model of the chip and the chip are the same object.

23
00:02:45,248 --> 00:02:51,567
A route VPR finds can always be loaded, and every mux in the hardware exists in the router's graph.

24
00:02:51,887 --> 00:02:59,414
If the architecture changes, the committed graph is stale on purpose, and every tool refuses to run until it is rebuilt.

25
00:03:00,967 --> 00:03:05,257
Counted, the graph has 9,928 nodes.

26
00:03:05,577 --> 00:03:14,475
Horizontal and vertical wire segments, block output and input pins, and inside each logic block the element inputs and carry nodes of its crossbar.

27
00:03:14,933 --> 00:03:25,664
8,095 of those nodes have a choice of drivers, so the fabric has 8,095 multiplexers and 28,782 select bits.

28
00:03:25,984 --> 00:03:33,609
The 138 direct connections, the carry chains and the DSP cascade, have no choice and so no bits.

29
00:03:35,167 --> 00:03:48,887
These are the files behind this chapter. device.py and the architecture writer are written by hand; the device file, the routing graph and the fabric are generated; and the device tests check that every mux encoding and every pin agree.

